A||||8||mrfixit58||Clutch Hydraulics 101||||8||
Z||000000||mrfixit58||02-28-2001||05:18 PM||||Now that we've beaten the hydraulic brake system to death, how about spending some time on the clutch system. One big gripe with my clutch is that the pedal throw is too long for my comfort. Many years ago, I used to own an import car (Who's name we'll hold back for now) and all I had to do to shift gears was to fan the clutch. Meaning, my heal would almost stay planted because I never had to depress the clutch all the way down to shift... only a little over half way.<P>My driving comfort is inhibited by the clutch in my Fiero. I'm more comfortable driving with the seat a little farther away from the steering wheel and my right leg extended. But, because I have to depress the clutch so far, I must sit closer to the wheel and bend my right leg more than I'd like.<P>So, now that I spilled my guts, I'd like to hear some suggestions other that bleeding the clutch. Can I move the pivot point on the pedal to use less throw? How about a larger bore clutch master cylindar? Or, smaller bore slave cylindar?<P>Suggestions, comments (or is it just me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ),<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000001||Formula88||02-28-2001||07:05 PM||||Hear, hear.  I'd love to see if I could shorten the clutch travel in my Formula!||||reg||1
Z||000002||MrPBody||02-28-2001||07:27 PM||||Shorten the clutch pedal arm?<P>Larger diameter master cylinder is probably the best bet, but not easy because you're trying to match mounting flange, outlet location and fitting size, and pushrod type/depth.<P>Shorter throw of course means greater effort.<P>Or maybe bolt a block (spacer) to the clutch pedal.  This doesn't decrease throw, but moves the pedal surface closer to you.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||vtail||02-28-2001||11:00 PM||||OK,<BR>I had the "clutch" problem with the 85 V6 4 speed I bought. After MUCH bleeding etc. I decided I need to get rid of the free-play in the clutch pedal. I took the pushrod going from the pedal and cut it in 1/2. I bought a 5/16" threaded coupler, threaded both ends of the pushrod and put a nut on each side to lock things in place. I then adjusted the length to take all of the play out but not putting preasure on the master cylinder. I now have a smooth working clutch that I do not have to push all the way to the floor to disengage. And I am now a happier person.||||reg||7
Z||000004||danny boy||02-28-2001||11:19 PM|||| Vtail, I was just gonna say that. I did it on my '86 coupe. It worked great. The clutch pedal sits out farther(closer to the seat) and you can put the seat anywhere you want to.It worked on my "Import in Disguise" as well.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stealth_feb2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||03-01-2001||08:39 AM||||Extending the clutch push rod sounds do-able. But, your pedels would then ne staggered like the old Ford trucks. Not that this is bad, just not real desirable.<P>Does anyone know where to go search for specifications of different clutch master cylindars?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000006||vtail||03-01-2001||03:36 PM||||That fix does not raise the pedal height. That is controlled by the pedal stop. All this does is let you adjust the length of the rod going to the master cylinder to take out ALL unwanted play. If you push down on your clutch pedal you will probably notice an inch or so of play before the rod starts to push in the clutch cylinder. I just removed that useless travel without changing the pedal resting point so that the pedal begins pushing on the clutch cylinder at an earlier point of travel.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||03-03-2001||12:23 AM||||hav a machine shop press in a smaller liner in to the stock bore the replace the piston with a smaller one and new seals i foun the the piston fron a honda wheel cylinder works fine with the rubber seals||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||03-03-2001||09:11 AM||||Bubba,<P>Are you talking about the master or the slave cylinder? Do you have the part numbers or the make and year of the Honda for the new seals and piston? What size sleeve did you install? What effect did it have on the pedal?<P>Sounds like a solid fix.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
